Date: Wed, 3 Nov 93 00:00:05 -0500
From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)
Subject: Future Science

>From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
>X-Message-Number: #93-11-63
>
>New thread: how will science be done in the future?  I submit that
>the next 20 years will drastically alter the face of science.
> ...
>GP can easily evolve designs and theories that no human understands.

I'm afraid that you're probably right.

>We have many gnawing mysteries in modern science, many of them
>baffling because of the subtle, complex models involved:
>the new gamma ray bursters discovered by GRO, the solar
>neutrino deficit, the source of heat in some cold "fusion"
>experiments, quantum dot phenomenon, the behavior of
>superconductors with imperfections, etc.  The
>rapid, automated evolution of complex nonlinear models
>will cut through many of these problems like a hot knife 
>through butter.  In the process, theorized phenomenon like
>wormholes, magnetic monopoles, etc. etc. may either be
>found or strongly falsified, as a new generation of GP-enabled
>automated labs takes to work out the vast complex of possible
>theories relating to these phenomenon, and in many cases to
>test them out with the automated laboratories of the 21st 
>century.

Unfortunately, the aims of science are not just to simulate and predict, but to
understand.  And a black-box approach like GP is likely to yield few
"explanations" (unless we are able to interpret the results of our evolved
simulation).  Call me old-fashioned, but I'd like to understand the
phenomena I study, not just mark them down as "solved".  Of course, science
survives primarily because it's useful, and the GP-driven science
you're predicting will probably be more useful than the traditional,
human-driven approach.  However, there's only so much you can do with
computational brute force.  To understand, and to thus wield greater control,
you must know _why_ your solution works, not merely _that_ it does.  This is
something most people who work with neural networks, who merely apply
"general" learning algorithms (which they do not understand) to any problem
they see, seem not to realize.

But I agree, genetic programming will probably cause a major shift in the way
science, or at least engineering (apply your own definitions), will progress.

Paul


