============================  Part  II  ===================================


   CF agent in practice                      [ November 2, 1993]
   ====================



    Mini-glossary:
    --------------

DF  - Directive ['dumb'] Filter
CF  - Collaborative Filter  (this term is wider than message rating)




   Note 1.    Don't feel miserable if you're under-rated.
   -----------------------------------------------------

   Ratings do not necessarily reflect the reader's opinion on the poster.
For example, I may rate Tim May's article on AIT with 0, because, with
all my admiration to Tim, his ideas on AIT are just too sophisticated for
me, and I do not want to read them.  I may rather read shorter and more
general remarks of some less advanced list member, and I want the system
to know it. Also, my ratings will encourage my non-professional tutor to
write more, and will send a signal to Tim May that he should not cast the
pearls of his intelligence under that Russian diletant (this is a purely
fictional example, of course ;-).




   Note 2.  Multiple domains.
   -------------------------

  The final implementation of the system should have separate topic
domains, since correlations of people's interests are different for
different topics.  You may respect my opinions on alien intelligence,
ignore my interest to food, and absolutely not want to share my
pathological interest to the messages with broken list commands that
I review for further software development.
  The software can be naturally extended from a single-domain system to
multiple-domain, with little or no alteration in user commands.
So for now I am dropping this subject (I have lots of ideas about it
and would be quite happy to share them with anybody who would be interested
in helping to implement them).
 


   Suggested user interface (Phase I)
   ----------------------------------


   0.  Limitations of Phase I.

   At this stage, *all* list will be considered a single topic domain,
and the ratings will not be turned into capital.  These two features,
unlike opinion correlations, don't seem to be crucial; besides, they
can be added later without restructuring the software and more or less
transparently for the user.



   1.  Rating commands:

   The ratings are integer numbers between -5 and 5
   (other range suggestions?)
   the [default] default is 0.


   Here are some examples that should make it clear:

   ::rate R             -- rates the message replied to
                      ("In-Reply-To" field should be supplied in this case)
   ::rate R #N          -- rates message number N
   ::rate R user        -- sets a default rating for author of current msg
   ::rate R user U      -- sets a default rating for a user U
   ::rate R user #N     -- sets a default rating for the author of msg #N
   ::rate R thread      -- sets a default rating for thread replied to
   ::rate R thread T    -- sets a default rating for thread T
   ::rate R thread #N   -- sets a default rating for thread of msg #N
   ::rate R all         -- sets a default rating for all messages.

   The word 'rate' may be omitted for speed of reply; I am not sure it
is necessary though, since it should take the user longer to think of an
appropriate rating than type the word 'rate'; besides, people will have
sufficient direct incentives to rate to justify typing 5 more characters. 
This can be left for the listadmin's discretion.
  My claim that this part is simpler than Tim May's suggestion is in that it
allows to set implicit thread/user/general ratings, which requires much less
input than individual ratings.


  2. Selection commands.
  ----------------------

     These commands are natural extensions of the DF agent's ::exclude
and ::include commands.

The following examples should be self-explanatory:

::include all 3.5       - send all messages rated over 3.5
::include all 0         - analogous to DF's ::include all; overrides the above
::include all 5         - no effect
::include thread FOOD 4 - "I'm not really interested in this,
                          unless the post is really good."
::include user Perry -5 - all Perry's messages
::include user Perry    - works the same as above; -5 threshold is implied
::include user -4       - I really liked the last post, and would like
                         to see more from it's author, except clear junk.
::include user #1983 4  - all future posts by the author of message #1983
                         rated over 4 by my agent.
::include top 10        - 10 highest-rated messages (for a period of time)
::exclude all -2        - ignore all messages rated *under* -2
::exclude all -5        - no effect


Priority rules maybe the same as with DF agent (user over thread over
'all').
  Since ::include and ::exclude commands for the  same threshold rating are
complimentary, and thus just one of them could be used, I would suggest to
have ::excludes have a higher priority in the same group category.
  My son's suggestion was that we just make the priorities of user/thread
include/exclude commands dependent on their order in the list; this is both
simpler to memorize and much more flexible than any pre-defined rules.
It's not very easy to shuffle the agent commands though.

3. Retrieval commands.

   Same as for DF, with an optional number indicating minimal rating
for batch ::resend's.   Default : -5 (send all).




   Calculations of ratings for the user.
   ------------------------------------

   All incoming ratings for a message are combined with various weights
to calculate the user's rating.  The weights depend on the correlation
of user's interests with each rater [in this domain] and also on the
reliability of this correlation (If I have a correlation of 0.7 with user
A, based on 124 read messages, and correlation 0.9 with user B, calculated
based on 3 messages, I'd put a bigger weight on the opinion of user A).
The correlations are calculated so that they can slowly drift as users'
interests change. The scheme for multiple domains is more sophisticated.
The nice part is that the user doesn't have to know anything about it.

    Without correlations, the scheme may not work at all, because of the
following reasons:

   - the ratings will not reflect *your* interests in any way, and will
     be in general a lot less accurate;
   - the poor ratings, and also the fact that the system will not be
     any better for me if I share my opinion, will not make the use
     of the whole thing too attractive.

  It is also remarkable that *with* the correlation of interests, people
who have never posted any message could benefit the list with their
ratings, will be stimulated to put more attention and thought into
reading, and will eventually be able to earn some Thornes just reading and
rating the posts. 

  The agent can suggest a menu of different methods for calculations of
ratings that would have different degrees of conservatism, computational
requirements, etc.   Writing and refining such methods will be another
money-making activity on the list.  Methods may charge usage fees.



 Message delays.
 ---------------

    We want the messages received to have accumulated some ratings.
This means that the messages should be delayed.
An additional consideration is that we do not want to lose the order
of messages.

   An ideal way to handle delays would be to have a hypertext with
some add-on software that would look around for the highest rated
unread messages and organize them into some quazi-chronological
threads.
  Until we have these capabilities, I see the following methods of
introducing delays:

   - introduce standard personal delays, e.g.:

     ::set delay 120      - mail will be sent to this user 2 hours
                            after it was received by the list.

     this is a very simple way of handling it, but it is hardly acceptable
     for frequent posters.  Lurkers may find it very convenient though.

   - set personalized reading schedule.
     e.g. if I read my mail only between 7 p.m. and 3 a.m.,
     the time between 3 am and 7 pm the messages can just sit
     there and accumulate ratings.  I can tell the agent that
     I 'sleep' between 3 am and 7 pm., as in:

     ::set sleep 3-19       [ list host's time ]

     - and the agent will not send anything to me while I 'sleep'.
     Also, if one day I decide to go to bed before 3 a.m., I can
     send a command ::good-night  to the agent, and it will hold
     further messages for me in the queue until tomorrow.

     this method can be easily combined with the delays.
     It is completely transparent to any user.

   - Send a bunch of messages on demand (when I log in).
     E.g. I can unsubscribe everything, and issue a
     ::[re]send new
     command that would send me everything that satisfies
     my selection criteria, starting from the first message
     I did not receive when I issued this command last time.
       This command would require a very quick response from
     the list (say, within 1 minute). Pay for it, or wait for it.


     With the last two ways of introducing delays, there are
   some issues to resolve at the ends of batches since most
   recent messages have not yet accumulated sufficiently reliable
   ratings.
     This can be done by using delays, or setting the period of time
   for which you want to see all messages, rated or not.
   If you want to just exclude all negatively rated messages, this is
   not a problem for you - you will receive all messages that have not
   yet disgusted your favorite raters.




     Another suggestion would be to just get the messages after they
  have surpassed a certain rating.  It has its own problems that I do
  not want to discuss at this point.

  My choices would be delays, schedule and direct request, combined.



  More on incentives and perspectives.
  -----------------------------------


    This scheme does provide you with an incentive to rate as many messages
as you can, and exactly at your opinion on them; this is the only way you
can make the system work for you (just like Robin's betting market).
    And with the number of people of the list exceeding a thousand or
two, this will be the only way to read it.  Your abilities are limited,
even if you are Perry.
    Without the message rating agent, we will have to close the list to
new members when the traffic becomes untolerably high, and will keep it
like that - closed and untolerable (after losing some of the best - and
busiest - people).
   So, message rating - or collapse of the list as we know it.


  Further Details.
  ---------------

  There are other issues to discuss here, including memory requirements for
keeping large correlation matrices, distributions of payments to authors of
messages and ratings, connections to HEx, access of the author to indivi-
dual ratings of people that they choose to keep open (I absolutely agree
with recent Hal Finney's remarks about the importance of feedback), etc.
I have more or less clear idea of how they should work, but for now will
spare typing effort and bandwidth. 

  Currently it's not the lack of ideas that is holding back the message
rating agent implementation.  It's the lack of support. If you think you
may benefit from new features in list software, consider taking part in
its development.



